{"author":{"id":"1832e4168d45d4ef31531146dbc743bb950a5dd543b15fd8e3a076fd976bfdef"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790960749,"nonce":"OK8lcps150l/ffbJaJFF7I9JmL4=","title":"Rayzor's method table names classes by the Rust namespace, its externs by the Haxe package","message":"`generate_rayzor*` gives each `NativeMethodDesc` the class `{namespace}::{Class}` from the namespace it is called with, while the Haxe surface annotates the class `@:native(\"{package}::{Class}\")` from the package `haxe` is called with. xgpu calls them with \"gpu\" and \"rayzor.gpu\", so the table registers `gpu.GpuDevice` while the externs name `rayzor.gpu.GpuDevice`. Rayzor matches the two only by exact name: the descriptors register a class nothing uses, and the methods work because Rayzor also registers bodyless `@:native` extern methods from the Haxe files. A descriptor and an extern meant for one method can then disagree without anything noticing.\n\nTake the Haxe package for the Rayzor class names, or check that the two agree. xwindow passes \"window\" to both, so its names match.","files":null}]}